  "order": 3,
  "title": "The note a chord is named after",
  "summary": "The bottom note of a stacked triad gives the chord its name.",
  "minutes": 4,
  "steps": [
    {
      "type": "explain",
      "title": "Root",
      "body": "The note a triad is built up from is its **root**. Stack 3rds on C and the root is C, so the chord is named after C.\n\n**Why it matters:** When a band mate says \"it's a G chord\", G is the root. Bass players usually play the root, so it is the note your left hand will want.",
      "exampleMidi": [
        60,
        64,
        67
      ],
      "labels": {
        "60": "C (root)",
        "64": "E ()",
        "67": "G ()"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "show",
      "title": "Roots of three triads",
      "body": "The roots are D, F and G: the lowest note of each stack.",
      "highlightMidi": [
        50,
        53,
        55
      ],
      "labels": {
        "50": "D: root",
        "53": "F: root",
        "55": "G: root"
      }
    },
